# ⛔ ONE LINE, AND ITS NARROWNESS IS THE POINT. Read this before adding a second path.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS. Every PR that ships a card appends its entry at the SAME anchor — the top of
# `[Unreleased]` in docs/CHANGELOG.md (VERSIONING.md § The `[Unreleased]` entry rule, gated by
# tests/changelog-card-entry-selftest.sh) — so every merge to `dev` leaves every open sibling PR
# CONFLICTING. Measured at five hand-resolutions in one session, and the cost is superlinear in
# parallelism: N open PRs mean each merge dirties N-1. A conflicted PR is worse than an annoying
# one — GitHub schedules no `pull_request` workflow when it cannot compute the merge commit, so
# its check set is `total_count: 0`, which is neither green nor red, and a naive "any failures?"
# read answers *no* (card#7227).
#
# ⛔ DO NOT WIDEN THIS TO ANOTHER PATH BY SYMMETRY. `union` is a line-concatenating driver with
# no idea what the lines mean, and two shapes this house's files actually have were measured to
# be DESTROYED by it:
#   * an append-only log whose entries carry a NUMERIC key (a decision log) — union interleaves
#     by position, silently producing the wrong order.
#   * one long table row edited on BOTH sides — union emits the row TWICE, and picking a side
#     drops whichever change lost. That one needed both semantic additions spliced into one row
#     by hand; no automatic rule could have produced it.
# The changelog is the one file here whose merge rule really is "keep both, in either order".
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT CHANGE. Every other path merges exactly as it did before, and a conflict in
# any of them still stops for a human. Nothing about the CHANGELOG's content rules moves either:
# the entry is still per-PR and still gated.
#
# TWO RESIDUALS, both MEASURED on this file's real structure, neither gated:
#   * UNION IS SILENT. A merged `[Unreleased]` is never presented for review, and it applies to
#     every three-way merge git performs — `merge`, `rebase`, `cherry-pick`, `revert`. Measured:
#     cherry-picking a commit that REWORDS an existing entry onto a branch that appended a new
#     one leaves BOTH spellings of the reworded entry, stale copy included. Read the merge
#     commit's diff of `[Unreleased]`; a clean merge is not a reviewed one.
#   * ORDER FOLLOWS MERGE DIRECTION, and one direction files an entry in the wrong section. With
#     a release rename (`[Unreleased]` → `[X.Y.Z]`) as OURS and a feature branch as THEIRS, the
#     incoming entry lands UNDER the version heading — well-formed, clean, and wrong; the
#     opposite direction is correct. So: merge `dev` INTO the feature branch, and never merge a
#     feature branch into `release/v<version>` — re-cut the release branch instead.
docs/CHANGELOG.md merge=union
